CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION

The sustained presence of human beings beyond the Earth-Moon system constitutes one of the most complex engineering challenges in the history of civilization. NASA's Moon to Mars architecture now formally incorporates a lunar surface cargo lander and an initial surface habitat, which must house astronauts on the lunar surface to extend crew size, range, and duration of exploration missions (NASA, 2024). These developments signal a transition from exploratory sorties to permanent settlement — a transition that demands a complete rethinking of habitat safety philosophy.

Current habitat safety systems were designed for conditions where Earth-based emergency services can respond within hours, where atmospheric pressure differentials are modest, and where a single structural failure does not immediately and irreversibly condemn all occupants. On the Moon or Mars, none of these assumptions hold. A micro-meteoroid penetrating a habitat wall at 20 km/s may cause catastrophic depressurization within seconds. A fire in a pure-oxygen-enriched environment may propagate through interconnected modules within minutes. A radiation storm may require immediate crew relocation to shielded refuges. In every one of these scenarios, human reaction time — even under optimal conditions — is insufficient.

This dissertation proposes and formally develops the Autonomous Habitat Isolation Gateway (AHIG): an architecture in which safety intelligence is distributed throughout the physical structure itself. Every pressure barrier — every door, airlock, and inter-module connector — becomes an autonomous sensor-actor node, capable of detecting hazards, predicting failure cascades, coordinating with neighboring nodes, and taking protective action faster than any human operator could.

The central research questions are: (1) Can the topology of a multi-module extraterrestrial habitat be formally modeled as a dynamic graph, and can optimal reconfiguration strategies be derived from this model under failure conditions? (2) Is it theoretically possible to predict habitat hazards before catastrophic failure occurs, using machine learning models operating on sensor data streams from distributed gateway nodes? (3) Can gateway decision-making be formalized as a Markov Decision Process with provably optimal policies, and what are the computational requirements for real-time policy execution? (4) Can formal verification methods provide safety guarantees for AHIG's distributed autonomous behavior? (5) What is the appropriate model of human-autonomy trust for life-critical autonomous decision-making in extraterrestrial environments, and how should AHIG balance autonomy and human oversight?

The original contributions are: a formal Habitat Graph Model (HGM); a Bayesian-LSTM Hybrid Predictor (BLHP) with physics-informed regularization; a Gateway Markov Decision Process (G-MDP) with performance guarantees; a Survival Bubble Formation Algorithm (SBFA) that is optimal for tree-structured habitats; a Fast Byzantine Consensus Protocol for gateways; a compositional verification framework using Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic (PCTL) and rely-guarantee reasoning; and a Human-Autonomy Trust Taxonomy (ATT) with a temporal authority model.

