CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE REVIEW

The field of extraterrestrial habitat design has matured significantly, but the dominant design philosophy remains anchored in passive structural safety. Regolith shielding, micrometeoroid protection, and structural redundancy have been extensively studied (ResearchGate, 2016; ScienceDirect, 2024). However, the question of what happens after structural integrity is compromised remains open. Existing work treats safety as either passive compliance, centralized monitoring, or single-subsystem automation. No existing architectural framework treats the entire interconnected network of module-separator elements as a distributed intelligent safety system capable of autonomous colony-wide reconfiguration.

NASA's Distributed Spacecraft Autonomy (DSA) project has demonstrated scalable autonomous coordination for satellite swarms, and the CADRE mission showcases multi-agent autonomy for lunar rovers (NASA, 2025; arXiv:2502.14803). These works establish the technical feasibility of distributed autonomous intelligence in space environments. Recent formal verification results for multi-agent path execution use MDPs and PCTL to ensure safety (ScienceDirect, 2025). In spacecraft anomaly detection, fixed-threshold methods are known to be insufficient, and machine learning approaches are emerging (arXiv:2008.08221). Digital twins are increasingly applied to space systems, but remain at the subsystem level (AIAA, 2024; ESA, 2024). Trust in autonomous safety-critical systems is a growing field, with programs like STARS focusing on safe trusted autonomy (arXiv:2501.05984). AHIG synthesizes these threads into a unified architecture.

