CHAPTER 4: THE AHIG ARCHITECTURE

AHIG rests on four principles: (1) Distributed Sovereignty — every gateway node is capable of autonomous survival-oriented decision-making without central control; (2) Predictive Primacy — the system acts on predictions of failure, not on detected failures; (3) Topological Adaptivity — the habitat’s connectivity graph is a design variable; gateway states are continuously optimizable; (4) Human Supremacy within Temporal Constraints — human override is absolute for actions with response windows longer than human capability; otherwise AHIG acts autonomously and notifies the crew.

The architecture comprises four subsystems: a dense sensor mesh co-located with gateways; a local intelligence engine (LIE) executing the BLHP, G-MDP policy, and consensus agent; a gateway actuation mechanism (GAM) capable of full closure in $<2$ s; and a redundant wired/wireless communication fabric (HIN) with latency $<5$ ms. AHIG operates in five modes (M0–M4) from quiescent to survival bubble isolation, triggered by colony-wide risk thresholds.

