CHAPTER 10: FORMAL VERIFICATION

We employ probabilistic model checking and compositional reasoning to prove critical safety properties.

System Model. Each gateway is modeled as a Probabilistic Timed Automaton (PTA) capturing its sensor sampling, AI inference, and actuation logic with stochastic failure and communication delays. The full AHIG system is the parallel composition of all gateway PTAs and the physical environment.

PCTL Properties. The following properties are formalized in Probabilistic Computation Tree Logic:

· P1 (No Crew Abandonment): $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.999}[ \text{true } \mathcal{U}^{\le T_{\text{evac}}} \text{ crew\_safe} ]$.
    With probability at least 0.999, all crew reach a safe module within the evacuation time.
· P2 (Hazard Containment): $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.9999}[ \text{hazard\_in}(v_i) \Rightarrow X^{\le \tau_{\text{seal}}} \text{ gateway\_sealed}(\partial v_i) ]$.
    A hazard in a module leads to sealing of all incident gateways within $\tau_{\text{seal}}$ seconds.
· P3 (Deadlock Freedom): $\mathbb{P}_{=1}[ \neg(\text{deadlock} \land \neg\text{all\_safe}) ]$.
· P4 (Livelock Freedom): $\mathbb{P}_{=1}[ \text{hazard\_detected} \Rightarrow \Diamond^{\le T_{\text{resolve}}} \text{hazard\_resolved} ]$.
· P5 (Human Override Acknowledgment): $\mathbb{P}_{\ge 0.9999}[ \text{human\_cmd}(a) \Rightarrow X^{\le \delta_{\text{ack}}} (\text{exec}(a) \lor \text{safety\_veto}(a)) ]$.

Compositional Verification. We adopt a rely‑guarantee approach. For each gateway $G_i$, we define an assumption $A_i$ about the behavior of its neighboring gateways (e.g., they will not simultaneously issue conflicting seal commands) and a guarantee $G_i$ that, if $A_i$ holds, $G_i$’s actions will satisfy the local projections of P1–P5. The global system safety is then established by proving that the parallel composition of all guarantees implies the overall PCTL properties, provided the bounded interference condition $\max_{i,j} \|\mathbf{r}_i - \mathbf{r}_j\|_\infty \le \delta$ holds. This condition limits the divergence of risk assessments across the colony, which is enforced by the consensus protocol.

Proof sketch. By induction on the communication rounds, if every gateway maintains its local guarantee under the neighbor assumption, then the ensemble cannot reach a state violating any global safety property, because any such violation would imply a violation of some local guarantee. Formal rely‑guarantee rules for probabilistic systems (as in the literature) justify this deduction. We have verified the local properties of a single gateway PTA using the PRISM model checker, and the compositional framework guarantees scalability. $\square$

