CHAPTER 11: DIGITAL TWIN INTEGRATION

The AHIG Digital Twin Architecture (ADTA) operates at three levels: gateway‑level, module‑level, and colony‑level. Each physical gateway $e_{ij}$ has a digital twin $\tilde{e}_{ij}$ that receives state updates at interval $\Delta t_{\text{sync}}$. The twin’s state evolves via:

\tilde{s}_{ij}(t) = \alpha_{\text{sync}} \cdot s_{ij}(t) + (1-\alpha_{\text{sync}}) \cdot f_{\text{physics}}(\tilde{s}_{ij}(t-\Delta t_{\text{sync}})),

where $\alpha_{\text{sync}} \in [0,1]$ balances measurement trust and model propagation. The colony‑level twin runs forward simulations to predict the future state $\tilde{\mathcal{G}}(t+T_{\text{predict}})$, enabling counterfactual analysis for crew decision support. Latency of the twin must be less than one‑third of the shortest hazard timescale to be actionable.

