CHAPTER 12: HUMAN‑AUTONOMY TRUST AND INTERACTION

Trust calibration is critical. We define four dimensions: Competence Trust $T_C$ (perceived accuracy of AHIG actions), Benevolence Trust $T_B$ (belief it prioritizes crew welfare), Predictability Trust $T_P$ (ability of crew to anticipate actions), and Transparency Trust $T_{TR}$ (comprehensibility of explanations). The aggregate trust index is a weighted sum.

AHIG’s Explainability Module generates a concise natural‑language justification for every autonomous action within 200 ms, including the physical rationale, confidence interval, and override instructions.

Temporal Authority Model. Human authority over a given action depends on the remaining time to catastrophe:

\mathcal{AUTH}_{\text{human}}(h,t) = \max\left(\mathcal{AUTH}_{\text{floor}},\; 1 - \frac{\tau_{h,\min}}{\delta_h^{\text{human}}}\right),

where $\mathcal{AUTH}_{\text{floor}} = 0.1$ is the minimum human authority, $\tau_{h,\min}$ is the remaining time to catastrophic threshold, and $\delta_h^{\text{human}}$ is the human response time for that hazard. When $\mathcal{AUTH}_{\text{human}} \ge 0.5$, AHIG recommends and awaits confirmation for up to $\tau_{h,\min}/2$ seconds before acting autonomously; below 0.5, AHIG acts immediately and notifies.

