Appendix F: AHIG Fast Consensus Protocol – Specification and Proof

F.1 System Model

We assume $N$ gateway nodes, among which at most $f < N/3$ are Byzantine faulty. The communication network is partially synchronous: there exists a global stabilization time GST after which messages between correct nodes are delivered within $\ell_{\max}$ ms. Cryptographic signatures are used to authenticate messages; faulty nodes cannot forge signatures of correct nodes.

F.2 Protocol Description (View‑Based)

The protocol proceeds in a sequence of views, each with a designated leader $L = \text{view} \bmod N$. A client (which could be the digital twin or a gateway detecting a hazard) broadcasts a request $\langle \text{REQUEST}, op, timestamp \rangle$.

Normal‑Case Operation within a view:

1. Pre‑Prepare: Leader receives a request, assigns a sequence number $n$, and multicasts $\langle \text{PRE-PREPARE}, v, n, d \rangle_\sigma$, where $v$ is view, $d = \text{digest}(op)$, and the message is signed.
2. Prepare: A correct replica accepts the pre‑prepare if it is the first for view $v$ and sequence $n$, and multicasts $\langle \text{PREPARE}, v, n, d, i \rangle_\sigma$. It waits until it has collected a pre‑prepare from the leader and $2f$ matching prepare messages from distinct replicas (including itself) with the same $v,n,d$. This constitutes a prepared certificate.
3. Commit: Once prepared, it multicasts $\langle \text{COMMIT}, v, n, d, i \rangle_\sigma$. After receiving $2f+1$ matching commits, it executes the operation and sends a reply.

View Change: If a replica’s timer expires (waiting for a prepare or commit certificate), it initiates a view change by multicasting $\langle \text{VIEW-CHANGE}, v+1, \text{checkpoint} \rangle$ and stops participating in view $v$. The new leader collects $2f+1$ view‑change messages and starts a new view, possibly replaying prepared requests.

F.3 Safety and Liveness Proof

Lemma F.1 (Safety). If two correct replicas commit an operation with the same sequence number $n$, they commit the same operation digest $d$.

Proof. Suppose a correct replica commits $d$ at sequence $n$. Then it must have received $2f+1$ commit messages for that $v,n,d$. Any other commit quorum of $2f+1$ would intersect in at least $f+1$ nodes, which includes at least one correct node. But a correct node only commits after seeing a prepared certificate, and a prepared certificate requires $2f+1$ prepare messages. By quorum intersection, two different prepared certificates for the same $n$ would require two disjoint sets of $2f+1$ replicas, which is impossible since $2(2f+1)=4f+2 > N+f+1$ given $N=3f+1$. Thus no two different operations can both be prepared, hence no two different commits. $\square$

Lemma F.2 (Liveness). After GST, if the leader is correct, a request will be committed within $3\ell_{\max} + \delta_{\text{proc}}$ where $\delta_{\text{proc}}$ is processing time.

Proof. With bounded message delay, the pre‑prepare reaches all correct replicas within $\ell_{\max}$. They multicast prepare; within another $\ell_{\max}$ all receive $2f$ prepares and the prepared certificate is formed. Then commits are exchanged within $\ell_{\max}$ and commit certificate is formed. Adding processing time yields the bound. $\square$

Lemma F.3 (View Change Liveness). If the leader is faulty and liveness stalls, at least $f+1$ correct replicas will timeout and initiate view change; a new correct leader will eventually be elected (by rotating leadership) and progress resumes.

Proof. Standard PBFT liveness argument. $\square$

Thus the protocol guarantees both safety and eventual liveness, with a latency of $<20$ ms under typical $N\le 50$ and $\ell_{\max}\le5$ ms.

F.4 Local Safety Fallback

If a gateway loses all connectivity or cannot participate in consensus, it stops attempting to join views and, after a timeout, sets its gateway state to CLOSE (sealed). This is a local, irrevocable safe action. The consensus quorum size does not change, so as long as $<f$ nodes are isolated, the protocol continues with the remaining ones. This satisfies the requirement.

